{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}Methodology{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<h1>Methodology</h1>
<section class="card prose"><h2>Fairness rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Each system is the project's latest release as shipped, with its own bundled DuckDB. The DuckDB version is shown next to results.</li>
    <li>One system runs at a time. Each database container gets the same cores and memory cap, the load generator its own cores, and the page cache is dropped before cold runs.</li>
    <li>Engine settings are sized to the container, not left at defaults. Every non-default setting is listed below with a reason, and correctness-relevant knobs are set explicitly.</li>
    <li>The same queries run everywhere with only mechanical dialect translation (diffs below). A query a system cannot run is recorded as a failure with the error, never rewritten by hand.</li>
    <li>Every result is checked against a reference; a wrong answer is a failure whatever its speed and is never drawn as a bar.</li>
    <li>Timeouts, errors, crashes and n/a cells are shown with their reasons. Nothing is dropped.</li>
    <li>Overhead against plain DuckDB compares only equal DuckDB versions. The headline <code>geomean_common</code> uses the queries every integration completed; each system's own <code>geomean</code> and how many queries went into it are shown beside it, because a system that times out on slow queries would otherwise score better.</li>
    <li>VillageSQL runs in its own track because it is a different design (see Track B).</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section class="card"><h2>Limits and run parameters</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table><tbody>
    <tr><th>Database CPU set</th><td>{{ m.limits.db_cpuset }}</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Runner CPU set</th><td>{{ m.limits.runner_cpuset }}</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Container memory</th><td>{{ m.limits.mem_gb }} GB</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Per-query timeout</th><td>{{ m.limits.timeout_s }} s</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Warm runs per query</th><td>{{ m.limits.warm_runs }}</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Concurrent streams</th><td>{{ m.limits.streams }}</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Scale factors</th><td>{{ m.limits.scale_factors }}</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <h3>Campaigns</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>SF</th><th>Limits</th><th>Timeout</th><th>Measures</th><th>Systems</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {% for c in m.campaigns %}<tr><td class="mono">{{ c.file }}</td><td>{{ c.sf }}</td><td>{{ c.limits.db_cpuset }} / {{ c.limits.runner_cpuset }} / {{ c.limits.mem_gb }} GB</td><td>{{ c.timeout_s or m.limits.timeout_s }}</td><td>{{ c.measures }}</td><td>{{ c.systems }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table></div>
</section>
<section class="card"><h2>Correctness tolerances</h2>
  <p>Results are compared order-independently. Integer columns must match exactly; other numeric cells match when
    <code>abs(a-b) &lt;= max(abs_tol, rel_tol * max(|a|, |b|))</code> with
    <code>rel_tol</code> = {{ m.tolerances.rel_tol }} and <code>abs_tol</code> = {{ m.tolerances.abs_tol }}. NULL equals only NULL, CHAR padding is ignored.
    Reference: InnoDB and PostgreSQL at SF1 and SF10, plain DuckDB at the newest version beyond, cross-checked against an older version.</p>
</section>
<section class="card"><h2>Host</h2>
  {% if m.hosts %}
  {% for h in m.hosts %}<div class="tablewrap"><table><tbody>{% for k, v in h.items() | sort %}<tr><th>{{ k }}</th><td>{{ v }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>{% endfor %}
  {% else %}<p>No host facts recorded yet; every result record carries CPU count, memory, kernel and Docker version.</p>{% endif %}
  <p class="note">Each record also stores the system version, image digest, bundled DuckDB version, config file and run date.</p>
</section>
<section class="card"><h2>Every non-default setting</h2>
  <p class="note">Rendered with the talk limits ({{ m.limits.mem_gb }} GB, 16 cores). Comments in the config files give the reason.</p>
  {% for g in m.settings %}
  <h3 id="settings-{{ g.dir }}">{{ g.label }}</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>Reason</th><th>File</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {% for s in g.rows %}<tr><th scope="row"><code>{{ s.key }}</code></th><td>{{ s.value }}</td><td>{{ s.reason }}</td><td class="mono">{{ s.file }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table></div>
  {% endfor %}
</section>
<section class="card"><h2>Dialect translation diffs</h2>
  <p class="note">Plain DuckDB, pg_duckdb and the Track B entry points use the canonical text. MySQL-family systems (including VillageSQL) and PostgreSQL get only these mechanical rewrites.</p>
  {% for d in m.dialects %}
  <details><summary>{{ d.dialect }}: {{ d.diffs | length }} of 22 queries change, {{ d.unchanged }} identical</summary>
    {% for n, text in d.diffs %}<pre><code>{{ text }}</code></pre>{% endfor %}
  </details>
  {% endfor %}
</section>
<section class="card prose"><h2>Measure notes</h2>{% for doc in m.docs %}{{ doc.html | safe }}{% endfor %}</section>
<section class="card"><h2>Rerun it yourself</h2>
<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/EvgeniyPatlan/duckdb-in-oltp-bench.git &amp;&amp; cd duckdb-in-oltp-bench
scripts/dev.sh dbench --version
scripts/build-images.sh
DBENCH_DATA=/srv/dbench/data scripts/dev.sh dbench datagen --sf 10
DBENCH_DATA=/srv/dbench/data DBENCH_RESULTS=/srv/dbench/results DBENCH_RUNNER_CPUSET=16-23 \
    scripts/dev.sh dbench campaign campaigns/gate-sf10.yaml
python -m sitegen.build --results /srv/dbench/results --out _site</code></pre>
  <p>A campaign resumes after any interruption. See <code>campaigns/README.md</code> for the smoke run and the SF100 talk campaign.</p>
</section>
{% endblock %}
